export const RQ_ANSWERS = {
 "RQ1": "Over eight models, the workflow's decompositions have significantly higher domain independence and business-capability purity than both baselines, and higher modularity than single-shot prompting, at the price of lower database transaction purity (significant against the autonomous agent). The modularity gain is partly by construction; the domain-alignment gains are not optimized and are the independent evidence, although the size of the DI gain depends on the model. Reference similarity is not significantly better⟪: the C2C-50 advantage suggested by our first, mostly single-run analysis does not hold with more runs and models⟫. All LLM arms outperform traditional tools in cyclic independence and in close correspondence to the reference.",
 "RQ2": "The workflow produced a valid decomposition in 159/160 runs of the primary analysis and in 94.4% of attempts over all seventeen models, significantly more often than single-shot prompting. Every decomposition it produced was valid, because completeness is guaranteed by construction; its failures were answers that could not be parsed. No approach is stable across repeated runs, even at a fixed seed, and the workflow varies the most.",
 "RQ3": "The workflow is significantly cheaper than both baselines: 37.2% fewer tokens than single-shot prompting, in every one of the 32 cells, and on average far fewer than the autonomous agent, with the least cost variation across models. Half of its tokens are spent on evidence construction.",
 "RQ4": "The decisions that matter most are deterministic: reference-free selection that never grades a candidate by its own construction (significant ablation effects on modularity, cyclic independence, domain independence, and close correspondence to reference services, positive with every model), and refinement by measurable local search instead of LLM self-critique, which raises structure and domain alignment at the expense of transaction purity. Multiple candidates did not yield better designs; their value is as a safeguard."
}
